    "# Quantum Machine Learning for Alzheimer's Disease Classification\n",
    "### Challenge 02 · Quantum Machine Learning | Healthcare\n",
    "\n",
    "**Objective:** Build a hybrid quantum-classical machine learning pipeline using a **Variational Quantum Classifier (VQC)** to distinguish between **Alzheimer's Disease (AD)** patients and **Healthy Controls (HC)** based on electrophysiological (EEG) biomarkers.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Clinical Context: Electrophysiological Signatures of Alzheimer's\n",
    "In clinical neuroscience, Alzheimer's disease causes progressive synaptic dysfunction and cortical disconnection. This manifests in the EEG as **\"EEG Slowing\"**:\n",
    "1. **Elevated Low-Frequency Power:** Significant increase in **Delta (0.5–4 Hz)** and **Theta (4–8 Hz)** band activities.\n",
    "2. **Suppressed High-Frequency Power:** Significant decrease in **Alpha (8–13 Hz)** and **Beta (13–30 Hz)** activities, particularly across temporal and parietal lobes.\n",
    "3. **Loss of Complexity:** Lower spectral entropy and reduced cross-channel phase coherence.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Quantum Advantage & Exploration Hypothesis\n",
    "Classical machine learning models like SVMs map features into higher-dimensional reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces (RKHS). In this hybrid QML approach:\n",
    "- We compress multi-channel EEG spectral features into an optimal $N=4$ qubit budget via Principal Component Analysis (PCA).\n",
    "- We use an entangling **$ZZ$-Feature Map** to encode these non-linear feature interactions into the exponential $2^4 = 16$-dimensional quantum state space.\n",
    "- A parameterized variational circuit (**EfficientSU2 ansatz**) is trained via classical optimization (COBYLA/SPSA) to minimize cross-entropy loss.\n",
    "- We compare the quantum classifier against classical baselines (RBF Support Vector Machine and Random Forest)."
   ]
  },
  {
